{
  "book": "JER",
  "chapter": 46,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-04f983938de0c31d": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-555",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Furbish the spears and put on the brigandines."
      },
      "c-50a0e057e95bc4dd": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-557",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The most of commentators now agree that amon, here rendered “multitude,” should be taken as a proper name, and left untranslated. The original is amon minno, “Amon of No.” By No is undoubtedly meant the celebrated Egyptian city of Thebes, which was situated on both sides of the N…"
      },
      "c-b4238618991dd41c": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-556",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Kardom was a name given to an ax which seems to have been used especially for cutting down trees, and is thought to have had a heavier head than other axes. It is mentioned in Judges 9:48; 1 Sam. 13:20 and Psalm 74:5."
      }
    },
